course: dictionary
lesson: 11550
title: Ample: more than enough
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ample means more than enough, or plenty. When you meet a word like this, look at the words around it for clues. «We had ample time to finish the puzzle before the bus came.» The sentence says there was time to spare, so ample must mean plenty.
explain: Ample can also talk about size. «The farm had an ample garden with room for a pond and a swing.» Here the garden is big enough, with room to spare. Try the swap test: put «plenty of» in place of ample. «The farm had a garden with plenty of room» still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: Ample is a slightly formal word. In everyday talk, people more often say «plenty of» or «lots of». Writers choose ample to sound a little more polished, so you will meet it in stories, school reports and maths problems.
fact: Ample means more than enough; plenty.
fact: Ample can also mean large in size, as in «an ample garden».
fact: «Ample time» means there is more time than you need.
fact: In maths, ample tells you there is more than needed, so there is no shortage.
fact: Ample is a formal word; «plenty of» and «lots of» are more common in everyday talk.
exercise: «We had ample time to finish the puzzle before the bus came.» What does ample mean here? | Very little time | Time to sleep | Late and rushed | *Plenty of time, more than we needed
exercise: «The shop had ample bread for everyone.» Find and copy the word that means more than enough. | bread | *ample | shop | everyone
exercise: «There is ample space for everyone to sit.» Which words could replace ample here? | *plenty of | very small | broken | empty
exercise: «The team had ample energy at the end of the match.» What does ample mean? | No energy at all | *More than enough energy | Lost energy in the rain
exercise: Which sentence uses ample to mean large in size? | *The lawn was ample, big enough for a whole football game. | We ate our lunch quickly. | The park was closed on Monday.
exam: «The bakery had ample flour, so the cakes never ran out.» What does ample mean? | Not any at all | *More than enough | Just a little | Burnt and spoiled
